WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.833
Self-correction.

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:07.167
When something fails, a good agent should try something different, not the same thing again.

3
00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:09.867
There are two different things here.

4
00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:12.278
A retry repeats the same action.

5
00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:16.500
A self-correction chooses a new action, informed by the failure.

6
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.982
Retries suit transient faults, like a network blip.

7
00:00:19.982 --> 00:00:24.100
Self-correction suits wrong arguments, bad code, or failed checks.

8
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.800
For the second kind, the error text goes into the context.

9
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.767
Production agents need both.

10
00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:31.967
Here is the loop.

11
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:33.833
The agent makes an attempt.

12
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:39.600
It fails, and we capture the signal: a tool error, a failed validation, or a judge score.

13
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:46.710
That signal goes into the context, and the model reasons over it, so the next attempt is materially different.

14
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.433
It is capped, and then escalated to a human.

15
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.467
Here is a code-writing agent that debugs itself.

16
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:56.800
Each prompt includes the error from the previous attempt.

17
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.057
When the code fails, we keep the end of the traceback.

18
00:01:00.057 --> 00:01:02.467
That is the signal going back into the loop.

19
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:05.800
After three distinct attempts, we hand it to a human.

20
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.400
Now the real scenario.

21
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.200
DONKI is the flakiest dependency, and about a third of calls time out or come back empty.

22
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.367
The agent reads the error and changes strategy.

23
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:24.867
A timeout gets a shorter date range, an empty body gets the cached table, and stale data gets an EONET cross-check.

24
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:31.200
The common mistake is a blind retry loop against a failure that is deterministic.

25
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.100
Feed the error back instead.

26
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:38.300
This is the interview story: point at the trace where attempt two differs from attempt one.

27
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.339
Let's recap.

28
00:01:40.339 --> 00:01:43.067
Retry repeats; self-correction changes.

29
00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:46.267
The failure signal re-enters the loop as context.

30
00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:50.933
Use backoff for transient faults and error-in-context for systematic ones.

31
00:01:51.233 --> 00:01:53.377
And cap the attempts, then escalate.

32
00:01:53.377 --> 00:01:55.700
Next up: human-in-the-loop checkpoints.
